  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 4.3. Increased capacity of member States to develop policies, legislation and other measures that are specifically aimed at facilitating the transition of enterprises to formality</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Within the framework of the Transition to Formality Action Plan (TFAP), the Jamaica Business Development Corporation (JBDC), operating under the aegis of the Ministry of Industry, Investment and Commerce (MIIC) and in collaboration with the  Ministry of Agriculture and Fisheries (MAF) and the Rural Agricultural Development Authority (RADA), commenced implementation of the &quot;Alpha version&quot;, a recurring programme for &quot;Formalising Operators in the Jamaican Agriculture and Fisheries Sectors&apos;. 
It builds and expands on regular activities performed by the JBDC, by specifically targeting agriculture-and-fisheries operators, as part of the response to address the disproportionately negative impact of the Covid-19 pandemic and national measures on informal actors in these sectors.
As part of steps to operationalise the TFAP, the programme combines various measures that aim to facilitate the formalization of micro/small informal enterprises operating in the target sectors, by addressing capacity building of operators in fundamental business management and sector-specific technical skills. It also facilitates the transition of persons and business units to the formal economy by ensuring participants&apos; business registration and compliance with other formal operational requirements such as certification of goods and services provided, external trade facilitation and registration of workers. For sustainability of beneficiaries&apos; progress along the continuum for formalization, the programme also provides business coaching and other targeted business support services. Robust tools/ assets (for selection, assessment, training and evaluation) created during this alpha version will be used in subsequent iterations of the national programme.
 With the contribution of RBSA, the ILO has provided substantial technical advice and assistance for the design and implementation of the JBDC programme. Training materials developed for the programme draw from existing documentation the ILO developed concerning business continuity in response to natural disasters and COVID-19. 
The ILO also supports the JBDC in the development of key partnerships and facilitation of inputs into the programme of representatives of key beneficiaries (unions and employers organisations etc.).</narrative>
   </description>
